					<h2 class="chapter-title" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2.25rem; text-align: left;">Executive Summary</h2>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<p style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #1e293b; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; text-wrap: pretty;">
							Burning mouth pain, where the gums, tongue, lips, and palate burn relentlessly, is typically diagnosed as Burning Mouth Syndrome (BMS) once tests return normal. It presents an exhausting puzzle because visual examinations show completely healthy tissue. While chronic oral burning can involve complex neurological factors, my eighteen-month investigation narrowed down oral acidity and Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASICs) as key peripheral drivers. This book documents that research, providing an observational, first-principles framework to investigate chemical triggers and microscopic dental pathways alongside established medical&nbsp;care.
						</p>

						<div style="margin-bottom: 1.4rem;">
							<div style="font-family: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">Part I &bull; Burning Mouth Pain in 2026</div>
							<p style="font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: #334155; margin: 0; text-wrap: pretty;">
								Epidemiological realities (over 80 million sufferers worldwide, 3:1 to 5:1 female ratio), daily symptom characteristics across the tongue, palate, and lips, and the crushing human toll of the 3-to-5 doctor diagnostic odyssey. Crucially, this part traces the 70-year history from psychiatric dismissal to Lauria's 2005 small-fiber biopsy proof of denuded axonal sprouts, culminating in the modern 2026 international diagnostic&nbsp;consensus.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
							<div style="font-family: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">Part II &bull; My Story (An N=1 Investigation)</div>
							<p style="font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: #334155; margin: 0; text-wrap: pretty;">
								A software engineer's chronological case history documenting a sudden 10/10 burning pain flare triggered by acidic craft beer and wine. It details clean radiographs, 50+ failed supplements, the discovery of the carnivore elimination trial (starving oral bacteria to halt lactic acid), identifying the 48-to-72-hour delayed latency wave, the 92-dentist clinical poll, Crown #19 micro-leakage, and immediate relief from Gluma tubule&nbsp;desensitizers.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

