			<section class="book-page" id="page-1-intro-1">
				<div id="chapter-2" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch1-intro" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch1-descriptors" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>

				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="chapter-opener-header">
						<div class="chapter-opener-num">Chapter 2</div>
						<h2 class="chapter-opener-title">Let’s First Describe the&nbsp;Pain</h2>
						<div class="chapter-opener-divider"></div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page" style="max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto;">
						<p style="line-height: 1.82; margin-bottom: 1.35rem; text-indent: 0; color: #1e293b;">
							Before we explore diagnostic codes, Latin classifications, or medical theories, let us begin where every patient begins: with the raw physical reality of what this feels like. When people sit across from a doctor or dentist, they are rarely searching for an academic debate. They are trying to find human words for an agonizing, invisible sensation that feels as if their mouth has been dipped in boiling oil or scrubbed raw with caustic chemicals.
						</p>

						<p style="line-height: 1.78; margin-bottom: 1.45rem; color: #1e293b;">
							Across global clinical registries, patient forums, and validated McGill Pain Questionnaire audits, patients describe their oral burning using remarkably consistent language. These are not vague psychosomatic aches: they represent distinct neuro-sensory patterns reflecting irritated peripheral nerve fibers:
						</p>

						<ul class="chapter-list" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>Scalding Hot Liquid / Severe Coffee Burn (82% of patients):</strong> The sensation of drinking boiling soup or searing the tongue on hot coffee, with the intense scald refusing to fade after days, weeks, or months.
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>Raw, Sandpaper, or Peeling Surface (74% of patients):</strong> The distinct feeling that the delicate top layer of the tongue has been stripped away, leaving exposed flesh rubbing against the teeth with every swallow.
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>Dry Sand / Parchment Mouth (67% of patients):</strong> An acute drying sensation where oral tissues stick painfully to the inner cheeks and palate, even when clinical saliva production measures within normal limits.
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>Metallic, Salty, or Bitter Phantom Taste (64% of patients):</strong> A persistent chemical dysgeusia, often resembling sucking on a copper penny or lingering battery acid, accompanying the daily burn.
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>Electric Buzzing, Tingling, or Formication (61% of patients):</strong> Ectopic nerve firing described as tiny electrical shocks, vibrations, or small insects crawling across the tip and lateral borders of the tongue.
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>Direct Chemical / Battery Acid Sting (58% of patients):</strong> A sharp, caustic bite triggered within minutes of consuming mild dietary acids, citrus fruits, wine, or tomato-based sauces.
							</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>1</span>
				</div>
			</section>

