						<h3 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a; margin-bottom: 0.45rem;">The Daily Rhythm of the Burn</h3>
						<p style="font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.7; color: #334155; margin-bottom: 0.85rem;">
							Unlike headaches or muscle spasms that throb intermittently, burning mouth follows a relentless diurnal rhythm. Over 86% of sufferers wake up with a quiet mouth or a faint 1/10 warmth. As the day progresses - fueled by talking, ambient salivary acidification, and meals - the sensation builds continuously, reaching an agonizing 7/10 or 8/10 peak between 5:00 p.m. and bedtime.
						</p>

						<p style="font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.7; color: #334155; margin: 0;">
							Now that we have put clear words to the pain, the next logical question arises: when you sit in a clinic and describe these exact symptoms, what medical and dental labels are you given?
						</p>
