				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="chapter-opener-header">
						<div class="chapter-opener-num">Chapter 4</div>
						<h2 class="chapter-title chapter-opener-title">Who Patients Go and See, and What They Ask and&nbsp;Say</h2>
						<div class="chapter-opener-divider"></div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>When oral burning erupts, almost nobody receives an accurate explanation on day one. Instead, patients begin an exhausting clinical odyssey. On standardized healthcare surveys, BMS sufferers consult an average of <strong>4.1 healthcare practitioners</strong> - with many seeing eight, nine, or ten different specialists over two to four years - before anyone even mentions burning mouth.</p>

						<p>Each specialist looks at your mouth through the specific lens of their training. Here are the <strong>Top 10 specialists</strong> patients see, along with the common, frustrating dialogues that unfold in clinic exam rooms:</p>

						<ol class="chapter-list" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>1. The General Dentist:</strong> <em>"Your teeth look great, no active cavities, and your gums are completely healthy. Maybe you're clenching at night or using a harsh toothpaste? Try switching to a sensitive toothpaste."</em> (The dentist is trained to find gross structural decay, not sub-micron cement micro-leaks or exposed dentinal tubule proton flow.)
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>2. The Family Physician / Primary Care Doctor:</strong> <em>"Your complete blood count, kidney panel, and liver enzymes are perfect. Have you had increased stress, anxiety, or life changes recently?"</em> (Standard blood tests evaluate gross systemic organ failure, not localized oral micro-environment acidity.)
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>3. The Ear, Nose &amp; Throat Specialist (ENT / Otolaryngologist):</strong> <em>"I passed a scope down your throat; your vocal cords and larynx are normal. You might have silent acid reflux (LPR). Take this prescription proton pump inhibitor (PPI) twice a day."</em> (When the PPI fails to cure tongue burning, the patient is left confused.)
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>4. The Oral &amp; Maxillofacial Surgeon:</strong> <em>"There are no oral tumors, cysts, suspicious lesions, or bone infections visible on panoramic X-ray or CBCT. This is not a surgical problem. I recommend seeing a neurologist."</em>
							</li>
							<li style="margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
								<strong>5. The Neurologist:</strong> <em>"Your brain and cranial nerve MRI is completely clean; there is no evidence of trigeminal neuralgia, multiple sclerosis, or stroke. You have Burning Mouth Syndrome. Let's start you on low-dose Amitriptyline, Gabapentin, or Clonazepam to numb the central sensory relay."</em>
							</li>
						</ol>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>5</span>
				</div>
			</section>

