<! -  Chapter 4: BMS History and Evolution (Page 1 of 3: From Glossodynia to Neuropathy)  - >
			<section class="book-page" id="page-3c">
				<div id="chapter-8-history" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="chapter-8" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="chapter-5" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="chapter-4" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="chapter-18" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch17-algorithm" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch17-consensus" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch17-histology" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div id="ch17-stigma" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>

				<div class="page-body-content">
										<div class="chapter-opener-header">
						<div class="chapter-opener-num">Chapter 8</div>
						<h2 class="chapter-title chapter-opener-title">BMS History and&nbsp;Evolution</h2>
						<div class="chapter-opener-divider"></div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>To appreciate how modern clinicians navigate the intricate diagnosis of Burning Mouth Syndrome, it is helpful to explore the historical journey of oral sensory science. For generations, since oral tissues consistently appear completely normal and healthy under standard visual examination, medicine lacked the microscopic tools to detect irritated small-fiber nerve endings or sub-micron acid triggers. As neuroscience and diagnostic technology evolved, dedicated researchers and clinicians progressively unlocked the neurobiological foundations of oral&nbsp;burning.</p>

						<h3>1900 to 1950: Early Clinical Documentation of&nbsp;"Glossodynia"</h3>
						<p>From 1900 to 1950, chronic oral burning was clinically documented under terms like <em>glossodynia</em> (painful tongue) or <em>stomatodynia</em> (painful mouth). In an era preceding electron microscopy and receptor pharmacology, physical inspection was limited to macroscopic tissue evaluations. Because the oral mucosa consistently presented as intact, healthy, and pink, early medical frameworks often categorized these sensations as functional or stress-related responses, reflecting the diagnostic boundaries of the era. This clinical curiosity ultimately inspired subsequent generations of oral medicine specialists to search for deeper neuro-sensory&nbsp;mechanisms.</p>

						<h3>1988 to 1996: The Lamey Clinical&nbsp;Triad</h3>
						<p>The landmark clinical classification of Burning Mouth Syndrome was established between 1988 and 1996 by Scottish oral medicine specialist Dr. Philip-John Lamey (University of Glasgow and School of Dentistry, Belfast) through foundational studies published in the <em>British Medical Journal</em> (Lamey &amp; Lamb 1988 <a href="#src-31" class="cite-ref">[31]</a>), the <em>British Dental Journal</em> (Lamey &amp; Lewis 1989 <a href="#src-31" class="cite-ref">[31]</a>), and the <em>Journal of Oral Pathology &amp; Medicine</em> (Lamey et al. 1994 <a href="#src-32" class="cite-ref">[32]</a>). Lamey introduced the first rigorous clinical taxonomy, dividing BMS into three diurnal subtypes based on how pain behaves from morning to&nbsp;night:</p>
						<ul class="chapter-list">
							<li><strong>Type 1 (33% to 35% of patients):</strong> Symptom-free upon waking. Burning sensations ignite by mid-morning and steadily climb to peak intensity by evening (Lamey &amp; Lewis <a href="#src-31" class="cite-ref">[31]</a>).</li>
							<li><strong>Type 2 (55% of patients):</strong> Continuous, unrelenting burning from the instant of awakening to sleep. Highly refractory (meaning stubbornly resistant to standard medical treatments), tracking severe baseline nerve hyperexcitability and chronic sleep disruption (Lamey <a href="#src-33" class="cite-ref">[33]</a>).</li>
							<li><strong>Type 3 (10% to 12% of patients):</strong> Intermittent, erratic burning with pain-free days, frequently localized to atypical mucosal areas and triggered by specific food contacts, additives, or episodic gastric reflux (Lamey et al. <a href="#src-32" class="cite-ref">[32]</a>).</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>1990s to 2004: Primary vs. Secondary BMS and&nbsp;ICHD-II</h3>
						<p>By 2004, the International Headache Society formally codified BMS in the <em>International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-II)</em> as code 13.18.5 <a href="#src-35" class="cite-ref">[35]</a>. Clinicians divided oral burning into two distinct categories, following standard medical logic where doctors must first rule out known, treatable diseases (Secondary) before labeling a patient with an unexplained condition (Primary):</p>
						<ul class="chapter-list">
							<li><strong>Secondary BMS (Investigated First):</strong> Oral burning caused directly by an identifiable, visible, or treatable medical condition, such as oral thrush (candidiasis), Sj&ouml;gren's dry mouth syndrome, uncontrolled diabetes, severe vitamin B12 or folate deficiency, or blood pressure medication side effects (Scala et al. <a href="#src-34" class="cite-ref">[34]</a>; Patton et al. <a href="#src-34" class="cite-ref">[34]</a>). When that specific underlying disease is treated, the burning sensation typically&nbsp;resolves.</li>
							<li><strong>Primary BMS (When No Other Cause Is Found):</strong> Idiopathic oral burning where all routine clinical examinations, blood panels, and oral tissue biopsies return completely normal and healthy (ICHD-II 2004; ICOP 2020 &sect;6.1.1 <a href="#src-35" class="cite-ref">[35]</a>). Because every standard test comes back spotless, conventional medicine viewed Primary BMS as an insoluble mystery with no visible physical target.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>14</span>
				</div>
