						<div id="ep3-cleaning" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h3>Episode #3: Routine Dental Cleaning (Mechanical &amp; Bacterial&nbsp;Shock)</h3>
						<p>Believing my oral hygiene might be contributing, I scheduled a routine dental prophylaxis. The hygienist performed standard ultrasonic scaling and debridement, followed by an antimicrobial chlorhexidine rinse. Within 48 hours, my mouth erupted into an intense 10/10 flare. This was not a reflection of hygiene technique, but of my underlying tissue vulnerability: ultrasonic vibrations can temporarily disrupt a fragile salivary pellicle on unshielded roots, while low-pH or alcohol-containing rinses can irritate exposed dentinal tubules and lower the firing threshold of dormant, sensitized nerve&nbsp;endings.</p>

						<h4 class="episode-subheading">What Patients Hear When Cleanings Trigger&nbsp;Pain</h4>
						<p>When an ultrasonic cleaning or antimicrobial rinse triggers an unexpected nerve flare, dental professionals naturally evaluate the mouth through standard clinical metrics: checking for calculus, bone loss, or bleeding. Finding none, they offer standard reassurances that, while clinically accurate for periodontal health, leave the patient's burning nerve pain unexplained:</p>
						<ul class="chapter-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.6rem;">
							<li><em>"Your gums look immaculate, there is zero clinical reason for pain."</em></li>
							<li><em>"It must be stress, anxiety, or clenching your jaw at night."</em></li>
							<li><em>"Try switching to a sensitive toothpaste and drink more cold water."</em></li>
							<li><em>"Your teeth and gums are structurally healthy, so this presentation suggests an underlying sensory or neurological&nbsp;pathway."</em></li>
						</ul>

						<div id="ch7-blind-spot" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The Interdisciplinary Care&nbsp;Boundary</h4>
						<p>This highlights a significant structural boundary in modern healthcare: dentistry focuses primarily on hard tissue pathology (caries, fractures) and periodontal attachment, while neurology focuses on central pain processing. Peripheral micro-chemical leaks at the tooth-mucosa margin sit directly at the intersection of both disciplines. Recognizing this interface creates an essential bridge, allowing dentists and physicians to combine restorative sealing with neurological support for comprehensive patient&nbsp;care.</p>
					</div>
