						<h3>Episode #5: Gumline Bonding Patches (Cervical Tubule&nbsp;Shields)</h3>
						<p>Suspecting that acid was penetrating through receding gumlines, I partnered with my dentist to place composite resin bonding patches over exposed cervical root margins. Shielding these open dentinal tubules provided immediate localized relief, reducing baseline pain from 8/10 down to 2-3/10 and confirming that open root dentin acts as a physical conduit transmitting oral acid directly to the dental pulp and trigeminal nerve&nbsp;endings.</p>

						<div id="ch5-patches-trap" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The Technique Sensitivity of Cervical Resin&nbsp;Bonding</h4>
						<p>Direct composite bonding at the cervical root margin requires exquisite moisture isolation and technique precision. In a humid oral environment, resin polymerization shrinkage, micro-voids during placement, or crevicular fluid interference can leave sub-micron marginal gaps where bacterial biofilm colonizes the adhesive interface. Trapped bacteria ferment carbohydrates directly against permeable dentin, creating a localized lactic acid micro-reservoir that activates adjacent ASIC channels and fires the nerve&nbsp;continuously.</p>

