						<h3>Episode #8: 3D Arch Mapping &amp; Crown #19 Removal (The Mechanical&nbsp;Leak)</h3>
						<p>While strict diet maintained a 0/10 baseline, reintroducing even minor carbohydrates still triggered burning in a localized sector: the lower left lingual quadrant. By conducting 3D arch mapping, I traced the epicenter of the burn directly to the lingual margin of tooth #19, an old porcelain-fused-to-metal (PFM) crown placed years&nbsp;prior.</p>

						<div id="ch5-second-opinion" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The Second Opinion Experiment: 92 Dental&nbsp;Professionals</h4>
						<p>I posted high-resolution bitewing X-rays of tooth #19 to clinical dental forums, asking 92 dental professionals if the crown looked clinically sound. When I shared high-resolution bitewing radiographs of tooth #19 with 92 experienced clinicians, over 85% noted that the margins appeared clinically acceptable on screen. This was not an oversight of clinical skill, but a powerful demonstration of the physical limits of 2D radiography: radio-opaque metal and zirconia restorations cast a solid white silhouette that physically masks early cement washout and recurrent decay along internal prep&nbsp;walls.</p>

						<div class="book-image-container" style="margin: 0.55rem 0 0.35rem; text-align: center;">
