			<!-- Chapter 7: The Biomechanical and Cellular Sources of Oral Acidity (Page 28-acid-2) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="page-28-acid-sources-2">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<div class="running-head-stamp-row">
							<button class="page-draft-stamp" type="button" title={`Active draft copy • Revised ${lastModifiedDate} • Click to view note`}><span class="stamp-line">DRAFT</span><span class="stamp-line">COPY</span><span class="stamp-date">{lastModifiedDate}</span></button>
						</div>
						<div class="running-head-text-row">
							<span class="book-title-header-label">Acid-Triggered Burning Mouth</span>
							<span class="chapter-header-label">Part III &bull; Chapter 7: Where Pain Can Come From</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>Biomechanical, Cellular &amp; Salivary Acid Generators</h3>
						<p>Beyond external diet, bacterial plaque, and nighttime reflux, the mouth harbors three profound internal and biomechanical acid-generating mechanisms that operate directly inside the periodontal socket and oral mucosa:</p>

						<ul class="chapter-list">
							<li><strong>4. Salivary Evaporation &amp; Buffer Collapse (Mouth Breathing &amp; Xerostomia):</strong> Resting saliva flow delivers continuous bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) to buffer oral acids, along with mucin coatings (MUC5B, MUC7) that shield delicate nerve endings. Chronic mouth breathing, nocturnal airway resistance, and medication-induced xerostomia rapidly evaporate the thin salivary aqueous film. As moisture vanishes, the concentration of residual hydrogen ions surges geometrically, collapsing local buffer capacity and allowing microscopic acid pockets to bathe unshielded mucosal C-fibers without dilution.</li>

							<li><strong>5. Osteoclast Proton Pumps (Active Bone Remodeling Under Shear Stress):</strong> When traumatic occlusion or grinding exerts heavy non-axial shear force against a tooth, the alveolar bone on the compression side must resorb to relieve stress. Bone resorption is an aggressively acidic chemical process:
								<ul style="margin: 0.35rem 0 0.45rem 1rem; line-height: 1.55;">
									<li><em>The Sealing Zone:</em> Specialized bone-resorbing cells (osteoclasts) latch onto the internal alveolar bone surface, forming an airtight ring known as the sealing zone.</li>
									<li><em>The Proton Engine (V-ATPase):</em> Inside this sealed chamber (Howship's lacuna), the osteoclast engages dedicated Vacuolar H<sup>+</sup>-ATPase (V-ATPase) pumps, blasting concentrated hydrogen protons directly onto the mineralized bone matrix.</li>
									<li><em>Extracellular pH 4.5:</em> Local pH directly beneath the osteoclast plunges to 4.5 to dissolve calcium hydroxyapatite crystals. Under persistent mechanical clenching, these proton pumps fire continuously, bathing adjacent periodontal sensory nerve terminals in persistent, localized acidity.</li>
								</ul>
							</li>

							<li><strong>6. Mechanical Ischemia &amp; Anaerobic Glycolytic Acid in the Periodontal Ligament (PDL):</strong> The fundamental principle governing acid generation from ligament compression is mechanical ischemia driving anaerobic glycolysis inside a non-compliant physical space. When non-axial bite forces hit the angled lingual slope of an upper canine, the tooth acts as a Class I lever:
								<ul style="margin: 0.35rem 0 0.45rem 1rem; line-height: 1.55;">
									<li><em>Capillary Perfusion Pressure Override:</em> Under healthy conditions, capillary blood perfuses the 0.2 mm wide periodontal ligament at 25 to 35 mmHg. Lateral grinding spikes interstitial tissue pressure above 200 to 300 mmHg, completely collapsing thin-walled capillaries like pinched drinking straws and cutting off oxygen delivery.</li>
									<li><em>The Anaerobic Glycolytic Switch:</em> Deprived of oxygen, ligament fibroblasts, cementoblasts, and endothelial cells switch from aerobic respiration to anaerobic glycolysis. Every single glucose molecule yields two lactate molecules and two free hydrogen protons (H<sup>+</sup>). Because squashed venules and lymphatics cannot carry protons away or import bicarbonate, interstitial tissue pH plummets from 7.4 to below 6.0:
										<div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 6px; padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; margin: 0.4rem 0; font-family: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: 0.82rem; color: #1e293b;">
											<strong>Aerobic:</strong> Glucose + 6 O<sub>2</sub> &rarr; 36 ATP + 6 CO<sub>2</sub> + 6 H<sub>2</sub>O (Neutral pH 7.4)<br />
											<strong>Ischemic:</strong> Glucose &rarr; 2 ATP + 2 Lactate + 2 H<sup>+</sup> (Acidic pH &lt; 6.0)
										</div>
									</li>
									<li><em>The Hyalinization Zone &amp; Macrophage Acid Blast:</em> Sustained nocturnal compression leads to sterile avascular collagen collapse (hyalinized zone). When blood trickles back upon relaxation, scavenger macrophages flood the necrotic zone, utilizing their own membrane V-type H<sup>+</sup>-ATPases to blast concentrated protons onto damaged collagen to dissolve it.</li>
									<li><em>The Non-Compliant Stone-Wall Trap:</em> Unlike skin or muscle, which can swell outward to dilute trapped acid, the 0.2 mm PDL is trapped between mineralized root dentin and cortical alveolar bone. Protons cannot escape; they sit directly against unmyelinated C-fibers and A&delta; endings, continuously locking open ASIC3 channels until mechanical stability and capillary perfusion are restored.</li>
								</ul>
							</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

