				</div>
			</section>


			<!-- Chapter 7: Where Pain Can Come From (Page 2 of 10: A-Delta vs. C-Fibers) -->

			<section class="book-page" id="page-28-fibers">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<div class="running-head-stamp-row">
							<button class="page-draft-stamp" type="button" title={`Active draft copy • Revised ${lastModifiedDate} • Click to view note`}><span class="stamp-line">DRAFT</span><span class="stamp-line">COPY</span><span class="stamp-date">{lastModifiedDate}</span></button>
						</div>
						<div class="running-head-text-row">
							<span class="book-title-header-label">Acid-Triggered Burning Mouth</span>
							<span class="chapter-header-label">Part III &bull; Chapter 7: Where Pain Can Come From</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Two Neural Cables: Sharp Zing vs. Slow Burn</h3>
						<p>To understand how oral pain is transmitted, we must look at the two distinct types of sensory nerve fibers wiring the teeth, gingiva, and oral&nbsp;mucosa:</p>

						<p><strong>The Normal Zing (A&delta; Fibers and Br&auml;nnstr&ouml;m's Hydrodynamic Theory):</strong> Thinly myelinated, rapid-conducting fibers (5 to 30 meters per second) located at the pulp-dentin border and inner third of dentinal tubules. Following Br&auml;nnstr&ouml;m's hydrodynamic theory <a href="#src-7" class="cite-ref">[7]</a>, fluid movements inside tubules mechanically stretch these A&delta; endings during cold or evaporative stimuli, generating a sharp, transient 2-second electric zing that stops immediately once the stimulus clears.</p>

						<p><strong>The Slow Burn (Unmyelinated C-Fibers: Pulpal vs. Mucosal Anatomy):</strong> Slow-conducting fibers (0.5 to 2.0 meters per second) that lack myelin insulation and convey burning pain, operating across two anatomically distinct zones:</p>
						<ul class="chapter-list">
							<li><em>Intradental Pulpal C-Fibers:</em> Located deep in the tooth pulp core, responding to pulpal inflammation or deep caries with a dull, throbbing toothache.</li>
							<li><em>Superficial Mucosal C-Fibers:</em> Innervating the tongue, palate, and inner lips via the lingual and mental nerves (trigeminal V<sub>2</sub> and V<sub>3</sub>). When surface epithelium thins, microscopic acid protons (H<sup>+</sup>) bathe these superficial endings directly.</li>
						</ul>

						<p><strong>Acid-Induced Peripheral Sensitization:</strong> When exposed cervical root collars or failing restoration margins create a continuous local acid reservoir, free protons directly gate acid-sensing ion channels (ASIC3) and TRPV1. This induces peripheral sensitization: lowering activation thresholds so dramatically that normal intraoral body temperature (37&deg;C) registers as scalding fire.</p>

						<h3>How to Explain the Tooth-to-Tongue Spread Accurately</h3>
						<p>A central puzzle in Burning Mouth Syndrome is why a focal tooth, crown, or margin issue triggers widespread mucosal and tongue burning. Because a tooth and the tongue receive sensory innervation from distinct branches (inferior alveolar nerve vs. lingual nerve), peripheral nerve cables cannot simply touch and jump signals in the jaw. Instead, neurophysiologists recognize two scientifically robust mechanisms that explain this spread:</p>

						<p><strong>1. Central Sensitization &amp; Dorsal Horn / Brainstem Cross-Excitation (Convergent Spillover):</strong> Sensory fibers from the inferior alveolar nerve (teeth) and lingual nerve (tongue) converge onto the same second-order wide-dynamic-range (WDR) neurons in the Trigeminal Subnucleus Caudalis: the brainstem's "dorsal horn." When an unsealed tooth margin fires continuous C-fiber volleys into the brainstem, it dumps substance P and glutamate, inducing central sensitization. These brainstem neurons become hyper-excitable and begin misinterpreting normal sensory signals from the tongue as scalding burning pain (referred pain and central sensitization).</p>

						<p><strong>2. The Direct Chemical / Micro-Environment Spillover (The Contact Model &amp; Remak Bundles):</strong> When a restoration margin or cervical collar is leaking, acid, bacterial plaque, and fermentable carbohydrates sit directly at the gumline. When the resting tongue presses against, rests on, or sweeps past that margin (lingual contact), the tongue's own mucosal C-fibers are directly bathed and triggered by the acidic micro-reservoir. Within the lingual mucosa itself, unmyelinated C-fibers travel bundled tightly inside common Schwann cell sheaths (Remak bundles). Sustained firing causes potassium ions (K<sup>+</sup>) to accumulate in extracellular gaps, creating local voltage fields that depolarize silent neighboring fibers (ephaptic transmission). This lateral electrical leakage causes activation to cascade across lingual C-fiber networks, radiating that focal contact burn across the entire lateral border of the tongue.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>28a</span>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Chapter 7: Where Pain Can Come From (Page 3 of 10: Master Catalog Part 1) -->

			<section class="book-page" id="page-28b">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<div class="running-head-stamp-row">
							<button class="page-draft-stamp" type="button" title={`Active draft copy • Revised ${lastModifiedDate} • Click to view note`}><span class="stamp-line">DRAFT</span><span class="stamp-line">COPY</span><span class="stamp-date">{lastModifiedDate}</span></button>
						</div>
						<div class="running-head-text-row">
							<span class="book-title-header-label">Acid-Triggered Burning Mouth</span>
							<span class="chapter-header-label">Part III &bull; Chapter 7: Where Pain Can Come From</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The 45 Receptors of Pain in the Mouth: Master&nbsp;Catalog</h3>
						<p>Across oral tissues and trigeminal nerves, chronic burning and hypersensitivity are driven by <strong>45 specific receptor types and ion channels</strong>. Below is the complete catalog with its core functional role:</p>

						<p><strong>1. Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC Subunits &amp; Regulatory Partners) [6 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>ASIC1a (pH 6.8 to 5.0, pH<sub>50</sub> ~6.2):</strong> Highly sensitive to physiological acidosis; calcium- and sodium-permeable. Senses initial drops below neutral pH, triggering rapid nociceptive depolarization.</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC1b (pH 6.5 to 4.5, pH<sub>50</sub> ~5.9):</strong> Expressed exclusively on peripheral sensory neurons. Responds to localized acidic ingress (pH 6.0 to 5.5), mediating sharp acid-evoked stinging and burning sensations.</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC2a (pH 5.5 to 4.0, pH<sub>50</sub> ~4.5):</strong> A low-affinity proton sensor that activates only under severe acidosis (below pH 5.0), modulating receptor heteromer kinetics and membrane stability.</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC2b (Non-proton-gated monomer; modulates pH sensitivity):</strong> Does not form functional homomers alone, but partners with ASIC1a and ASIC3 in heterotrimers, shifting activation curves toward neutral physiological pH.</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC3 (pH 7.2 to 6.2, pH<sub>50</sub> ~6.6 to 6.7):</strong> The most acid-sensitive channel in the human body. Senses the slightest drop from neutral saliva (pH 7.2). Lacks a rapid desensitization hinge, pouring an unbroken "window current" during persistent mild acidity (pH 6.2 to 6.7).</li>
							<li><strong>ASIC4 (Non-proton-gated structural chaperone):</strong> Insensitive to direct proton changes; acts as a critical regulatory subunit modulating trafficking, surface stability, and membrane assembly of active proton-sensing complexes.</li>
						</ul>

						<p><strong>2. Transient Receptor Potential Channels (TRP Superfamily) [9 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>TRPV1:</strong> Detects noxious scalding heat above 43&deg;C, capsaicin, and acid protons, dropping its trigger threshold to resting body temperature when inflamed.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPV2:</strong> Activates at high noxious heat thresholds above 52&deg;C and responds to mechanical membrane stretch.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPV3:</strong> Senses pleasant warmth between 32&deg;C and 39&deg;C and participates in oral epithelial barrier maintenance.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPV4:</strong> Detects mechanical cellular shearing, osmotic swelling, and tissue distension across mucosal surfaces.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPM8:</strong> The primary sensor for cooling below 26&deg;C and menthol, which triggers paradoxical burning when hyper-sensitized.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPA1:</strong> An environmental hazard sensor that fires in response to bacterial toxins, reactive oxygen species, cinnamon, and harsh toothpaste detergents.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPC1:</strong> A store-operated calcium channel that maintains baseline intracellular signaling and mechanical tension.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPC4:</strong> A calcium-permeable channel involved in microvascular tone and endothelial barrier regulation.</li>
							<li><strong>TRPC5:</strong> A specialized cold and dentinal pressure sensor highly concentrated on odontoblasts inside tooth tubules.</li>
						</ul>

						<p><strong>3. Hydrodynamic &amp; Mechanosensitive Receptors [5 Types]:</strong></p>
						<ul class="discovery-timeline-list" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
							<li><strong>Piezo1:</strong> A massive trimeric propeller channel that converts hydrodynamic fluid shear into instant electrical depolarization.</li>
							<li><strong>Piezo2:</strong> A rapid-adapting mechanical sensor that reports light mucosal touch and proprioception along tooth sockets.</li>
							<li><strong>TREK-1:</strong> A two-pore-domain potassium channel that allows potassium leakage to keep resting nerve membranes calm and quiet.</li>
							<li><strong>TREK-2:</strong> A mechanosensitive potassium leak channel activated by membrane stretch and intracellular acidification.</li>
							<li><strong>TRAAK:</strong> A lipid-sensing potassium channel that opposes mechanical nerve over-excitation in dental pulp fibers.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

