			<section class="book-page" id="page-30c">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<div class="running-head-stamp-row">
							<button class="page-draft-stamp" type="button" title={`Active draft copy • Revised ${lastModifiedDate} • Click to view note`}><span class="stamp-line">DRAFT</span><span class="stamp-line">COPY</span><span class="stamp-date">{lastModifiedDate}</span></button>
						</div>
						<div class="running-head-text-row">
							<span class="book-title-header-label">Acid-Triggered Burning Mouth</span>
							<span class="chapter-header-label">Part III &bull; Chapter 7: Where Pain Can Come From</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>How Burning Mouth Typically Starts: The Spark vs. The Loaded System</h3>
						<p>Ask ten people with Burning Mouth Syndrome when their nightmare began, and nine will name a specific calendar date: <em>"It started three days after my lower molar was pulled,"</em> or <em>"It hit the week after my car accident,"</em> or <em>"It slowly started creeping up after an old filling cracked and my gums receded."</em></p>

						<p>The human brain is wired for immediate cause-and-effect narratives. When a violent, ten-out-of-ten fire erupts in your mouth, you naturally blame the last physical event that occurred before the pain. Sufferers frequently carry a heavy burden of retrospective regret: <em>"If only I hadn't had that tooth pulled,"</em> or <em>"If only I had fixed that cracked filling earlier."</em> Yet materials science and sensory neurobiology reveal that dental procedures or aging tooth structures do not create BMS in an otherwise pristine biological system. You did nothing wrong; that immediate trigger was simply the final event that brought decades of silent cement hydrolysis, receded root margins, and microscopic tubule exposure to a sensory&nbsp;threshold.</p>

						<h3>The Illusion of the First Episode: The Match vs. The Dry Forest</h3>
						<p>Imagine a wooden house whose structural beams have been silently hollowed out by wood-boring beetles for thirty years, while the basement floorboards are soaked in fuel. If someone accidentally drops a lit match in the hallway, the entire structure erupts in an instant inferno.</p>

						<p>Did the single match burn down the house? Technically, yes, it supplied the initial spark. But focusing entirely on the match misses the biological reality: the catastrophe was only possible because decades of microscopic hardware leaks, exposed dentinal tubules, and salivary buffer depletion had loaded the system to 99% of its firing&nbsp;threshold.</p>

						<p>Across patient reports and clinical histories, symptom onset commonly clusters around three distinct clinical patterns:</p>

						<h3>Scenario 1: After a Tooth Extraction or Dental Surgery</h3>
						<p>A frequent subset of patients trace the acute onset of oral burning directly to an invasive extraction, implant placement, or deep root procedure. When patients return to the clinic, the dentist inspects the surgical site, observes healthy pink tissue healing normally over the bone, and confirms the socket itself is structurally healing without infection. Because localized tissue healing looks excellent, connecting this to burning sensations across the tongue or palate is difficult in a routine post-op check. The patient often leaves feeling bewildered. Yet from a sensory neurobiology standpoint, tooth removal initiates subtle biological processes that extend beyond the healed&nbsp;socket:</p>
						<p><strong>1. Interruption of the Apical Neurovascular Bundle:</strong> Routine dental extraction involves socket expansion and elevation to release the periodontal ligament. As the apex clears the socket floor, the terminal neurovascular fibrils that innervated the pulp are naturally severed at the apical foramen.</p>
						<p><strong>2. Traumatic Neuroma Formation:</strong> When severed nerve stumps attempt to regenerate in healing bone, they sprout chaotic axonal growth cones. Trapped in dense fibrous scar tissue, these sprouts form a traumatic neuroma, clustering voltage-gated sodium channels (Na<sub>v</sub>1.7, Na<sub>v</sub>1.8) that fire spontaneous ectopic electrical bursts into the jawbone.</p>
						<p><strong>3. Micro-Structural Shifts in Neighboring Teeth:</strong> Following tooth removal, normal alveolar bone remodeling occurs. As crestal tissue and gingival margins adapt, subtle root exposure can occur on adjacent teeth, unshielding 20,000 to 45,000 microscopic dentinal tubules per square millimeter beside the healing site. Ambient food acids can now enter these open conduits, introducing chemical stimuli to an already sensitized trigeminal&nbsp;pathway.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

