							<img src="/acid-sensing-ion-channels-asic-nerve.jpg" alt="Acid-Sensing Ion Channel (ASIC) Molecular Receptor Mechanism in Oral Nerve Endings showing proton gating" loading="lazy" />
							<figcaption class="book-figure-caption">
								<strong>Figure 2: Molecular ASIC Gating Mechanism.</strong> In sensory C-fibers, Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3) remain closed at neutral salivary pH (7.0). When local extracellular pH drops below 6.8, hydrogen protons (H<sup>+</sup>) bind to the channel's acidic pocket, triggering an immediate conformational shift that opens the pore and generates rapid inward sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>) currents that depolarize the nerve and project burning pain to the&nbsp;brainstem.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>
