			<!-- Chapter 12: The Importance of Documenting (Page 1 of 3: Tracking Philosophy & Core Data) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="page-15c">
				<div id="chapter-12" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="chapter-opener-header">
						<div class="chapter-opener-num">Chapter 12</div>
						<h2 class="chapter-title chapter-opener-title">The Importance of&nbsp;Documenting</h2>
						<div class="chapter-opener-divider"></div>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>To execute the 4-step master strategy successfully, you cannot rely on human memory. Memory blurs delayed timelines, magnifies emotional distress, and defaults to blaming your most recent meal. In neurophysiology, where bacterial fermentation operates on a 48 to 72-hour delay, unrecorded impressions are worse than useless: they actively misdirect you toward false culprits.</p>

						<h3>1. The Core Tracking Data: What to Log Daily</h3>
						<p>Your daily log does not need to be complicated, but it must be mathematically consistent. Track these four biological parameters every morning, afternoon, and&nbsp;evening:</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Pain Intensity (VAS 0 to 10):</strong> Rate your burning on a standard Visual Analog Scale. Record your baseline upon waking (which reflects overnight saliva stagnation and pocket fermentation) and note any progression toward the evening.</li>
							<li><strong>Resting Saliva pH (Hydrion / Litmus Strips):</strong> Test your resting saliva pH immediately upon waking and before meals using narrow-range pH strips (pH 5.5 to 8.0). Healthy resting saliva sits between 6.8 and 7.4. When resting pH drops below 6.5, Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC3) remain locked open in a continuous window current.</li>
							<li><strong>Exact Anatomical Locations:</strong> Detail whether the fire is isolated to the anterior tongue tip, bilateral lateral borders, hard palate, lower lip, or specific gingival margins. Tracking shifts reveals whether triggers are localized or spreading through referred cross-talk.</li>
							<li><strong>Detailed Nutritional Ingress:</strong> Record every food, beverage, condiment, and oral supplement with precise timestamps. Always note hidden culinary acids: vinegars, citrus marinades, hot sauces, coffee, and carbonated beverages.</li>
							<li><strong>Mechanical and Dental Events:</strong> Log aggressive toothbrushing, flossing sessions, dental cleanings, restorative adjustments, or periods of jaw clenching and nighttime bruxism.</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>2. The ABAB Single-Case Experimental Design (SCED)</h3>
						<p>To conclusively prove whether an input is a genuine trigger or a coincidental correlation, document your tests using formal Single-Case Experimental Design (ABAB&nbsp;testing):</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Baseline (A):</strong> Document your stable pain score and flare frequency under your normal daily routine for 7 to 14 days.</li>
							<li><strong>Intervention (B):</strong> Eliminate a suspected input (for example, coffee, wine, or all high-acid foods) or introduce an alkalizing protocol. Track the biological response over 14 to 21 days until a new baseline emerges.</li>
							<li><strong>Reversal (A):</strong> Re-introduce the specific input in a controlled manner. If the exact same burning pattern re-emerges within your documented 72-hour latency window, you have established strong biological causality.</li>
							<li><strong>Re-Intervention (B):</strong> Remove the input once again. When the fire settles back down, you possess rigorous, reproducible evidence that eliminates all subjective doubt.</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

