						<h3>3. Navigating Family Meals, Social Dinners &amp; Dietary Boundaries</h3>
						<p>Sharing food is the universal language of human connection. Family holidays, birthday dinners, and weekend barbecues are woven around eating. When your protocol requires eliminating acidic beverages, salad dressings, tomato sauces, or coffee, mealtime quickly becomes an emotional minefield.</p>
						<p>Loved ones often feel personal rejection when you decline their home-cooked meal, or they roll their eyes when you bring your own plain steak or neutral water to a family gathering. To eliminate this friction, establish clear protocols before you sit at the table:</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Announce the Elimination as a Temporary Scientific Trial:</strong> Frame your diet not as a permanent lifestyle demand or picky preference, but as an active diagnostic experiment: <em>"My dental specialist and I are testing whether cutting out food acids for 14 days stops my nerve flares. I am strictly testing this as a medical trial."</em> People respect medical trials far more than self-imposed food rules.</li>
							<li><strong>Decouple Food from Affection:</strong> Remind family members that declining their lasagna or wine is not a rejection of their hospitality: <em>"I love being here with you, and I love your cooking. Right now, food acids trigger intense nerve pain in my mouth. I am eating beforehand so I can focus completely on enjoying our conversation."</em></li>
							<li><strong>Bring Your Own Safe Solutions:</strong> Never demand that the entire household cook around your restrictions. Prepare your own compliant meals in advance, stock your own buffered spring water, and remove the burden of accommodation from your host.</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>4. The Support Contract: Turning Skepticism into Partnership</h3>
						<p>Your loved ones do not need to become neuroscientists, but they do need a structured way to support you without feeling overwhelmed by chronic illness caregiving. Establish a simple three-part <strong>Family Support Contract</strong>:</p>

						<div class="book-callout-box" style="margin: 1rem 0;">
							<div class="book-callout-icon">🤝</div>
							<div class="book-callout-text">
								<p><strong>Part 1: Validation Without Solutions:</strong> Ask them to stop searching Google for miracle herbal rinses. Tell them: <em>"When I am having a severe evening spike, I do not need you to fix it. I just need you to say, 'I know you are hurting, and I am right here with you.'"</em></p>
								<p><strong>Part 2: Objective Tracking Assistance:</strong> Invite a spouse or close friend to review your 14-day daily tracking sheet. When they see cold numerical logs connecting yesterday's acidic drink to today's 2 PM spike, their doubt evaporates. They see the data with their own eyes.</p>
								<p><strong>Part 3: Guarding the Evening Wind-Down:</strong> Because circadian cortisol drops in the late afternoon, BMS pain almost universally peaks between 4 PM and 9 PM. Agree on a quiet household routine during these hours so your nervous system can down-regulate without sensory overload.</p>
							</div>
						</div>

						<p>When you replace defensive arguments with clear biological models, objective tracking data, and practical boundaries, your family stops questioning your reality. They become your strongest allies on the road to permanent remission.</p>
