					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The Two Gateways: Systemic Microvascular Ischemia vs. Localized Microbial Acid</h3>
						<p>If diabetic neuropathy and BMS share identical sensory wiring, why does one burn in the feet while the other burns in the mouth? The difference lies in the route of metabolic&nbsp;stress:</p>

						<p><strong>1. The Systemic Gateway (Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy):</strong> Chronic hyperglycemia and Advanced Glycation End-products (AGEs) damage the micro-capillaries (vasa nervorum) supplying peripheral nerves, while intracellular sorbitol accumulation generates severe oxidative stress. Deprived of oxygen and nutrients, long unmyelinated fibers in the feet undergo mitochondrial exhaustion, ischemic hypoxia, and local tissue acidosis, driving continuous spontaneous firing of Na<sub>v</sub>1.7 and TRPV1 sensory&nbsp;channels.</p>

						<p><strong>2. The Local Gateway (Burning Mouth as a Localized Peripheral Neuropathy):</strong> In BMS, systemic blood glucose and vascular markers are frequently normal. Systemic microvascular failure is not required when open dentinal tubules and deep subgingival margins provide a direct physical conduit between oral chemistry and pulpal/mucosal C-fibers. Trapped carbohydrates are fermented by oral biofilm into localized lactic and organic acids against unshielded tooth collars. Instead of nerves being starved internally from occluded capillaries, sensory terminals are bathed externally by localized microbial acid&nbsp;pools.</p>

