					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>2. Why does Burning Mouth Syndrome so frequently start around 50 years&nbsp;old?</h3>
						<p>The fire does not materialize out of thin air; it is the culmination of a slow-motion mechanical and biological convergence fifty years in the making. In childhood and adolescence, chronic mouth breathing and juvenile decay lead to vaulted palates, dental crowding, and first-generation fillings, initiating subtle nightly drying. Through your twenties and thirties, clenching, crowns, and natural gum recession unshield 20,000 to 45,000 microscopic dentinal tubules per square millimeter along root collars. Meanwhile, restorative dental cements have a finite lifespan of 15 to 25 years before salivary acids dissolve their sub-micron&nbsp;margins.</p>
						<p><strong>The Midlife Occlusal Shift (Canine Guidance &amp; Abfraction in the 40s and 50s):</strong> A critical mechanical factor that peaks during this exact window is cumulative occlusal fatigue on the canines. In our 20s and 30s, resilient periodontal ligaments, youthful bone density, and thick protective enamel easily absorb chewing and nocturnal clenching forces. But by the 40s and 50s, after decades of lateral grinding cycles, the teeth experience severe structural fatigue. Because canines bear the primary lateral load during jaw excursion, they act as mechanical fulcrums. The constant bending forces cause cervical abfraction: microscopic crystal flakes pop off the tooth collar at the gumline, exposing tens of thousands of fluid-filled tubules. Combined with thinner labial bone plates and natural middle-age gum recession, an upper canine becomes an unshielded portal. Every clench acts as a hydraulic piston, forcing acidic oral fluids deep into pulpal nerve endings and launching an unremitting neuropathic storm.</p>
						<p>At age fifty, these mechanical and biological vulnerability curves collide: cumulative hardware wear, canine abfraction, and tubule exposure reach their peak surface area at the exact moment natural aging and perimenopause reduce resting salivary volume and bicarbonate buffer capacity. When the mouth loses its chemical buffer right as structural defenses are breached, everyday acids flood into open tubules, locking pulpal Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC3 and TRPV1) into continuous neuropathic&nbsp;firing.</p>
						<div class="book-callout">
							<span class="book-callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">
								<svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
