					<div class="chapter-content">
						<div id="ch30-diurnal" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h3>6. Why do some people burn far less in the morning, while others burn&nbsp;consistently?</h3>
						<p>This diurnal divergence is one of the most revealing clinical clues in oral neurophysiology. In the classic Lamey-Lewis classification, patients who wake with minimal or zero pain and peak by evening are categorized as <strong>Type 1 BMS</strong> (roughly 50% to 65% of cases). Those whose burning is unrelenting from the moment they open their eyes straight through bedtime are categorized as <strong>Type 2 BMS</strong> (roughly 25% to 35%).</p>
						<p>From a first-principles perspective, whether you burn less in the morning or burn consistently depends on the competition between <strong>overnight resting repair</strong> and <strong>continuous background triggers</strong> across three primary systems:</p>

						<p><strong>1. Salivary Clearance vs. Nocturnal Xerostomia and Mouth Breathing:</strong><br />
						During undisturbed sleep, waking mechanical friction ceases and you ingest zero dietary acids. If you breathe exclusively through your nose with adequate basal salivary mucins, your tongue rests in a quiescent, sealed biological bath. Tissues rehydrate, surface protons diffuse away, and you wake with calm mucosal tissue.</p>
						<p>Conversely, patients who burn consistently upon waking almost invariably experience <strong>nocturnal desiccation</strong>. Mouth breathing, CPAP air leaks, or medications with anticholinergic side effects (SSRIs, antihistamines, blood pressure drugs) dry out the oral chamber overnight. Resting salivary flow drops to near zero, stripping the protective mucin gel coat (MUC5B) and leaving the epithelial surface parched. When dawn arrives, small-fiber nociceptors have spent eight straight hours exposed to air friction and concentrated salivary electrolytes without a protective buffer.</p>

						<p><strong>2. Synaptic Recovery vs. Central Sensitization and Wind-Up:</strong><br />
						For those who burn less in the morning, overnight rest allows central second-order neurons in the trigeminal brainstem (subnucleus caudalis) to reset. Astrocytic glutamate transporter pumps (GLT-1 and EAAT2) clear residual excitatory neurotransmitters from the synaptic cleft. Only after hours of daytime speech friction, mastication, and dietary exposure does the synaptic cleft become saturated, triggering late-day <strong>temporal summation (wind-up)</strong>.</p>
						<p>In continuous, morning-to-night burn patients, the central nervous system has entered an established state of <strong>autonomous central sensitization</strong>. The dorsal horn interneurons maintain a lowered firing threshold that persists independent of immediate daytime input. The volume knob on pain processing remains turned to maximum even while asleep.</p>

						<p><strong>3. Intermittent Dietary Acid vs. Fixed Hardware Leaks:</strong><br />
						Patients with the morning-calm curve are typically reacting to cumulative daily exposures: morning coffee (pH 4.8 to 5.2), dietary acids, carbonation, and daytime clenching. Every acidic beverage introduces hydrogen ions (H<sup>+</sup>) that trip <strong>Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3)</strong>, gradually building a fire across the day.</p>
						<p>In contrast, people with consistent 24/7 burning often have a fixed, non-fluctuating hardware defect. A micro-leaking crown with dissolved cement, an exposed dentinal cervical defect bathed in persistent gingival crevicular fluid, galvanic current between dissimilar dental metals, or chronic silent nocturnal laryngopharyngeal acid reflux (LPR) provides an unrelenting peripheral stimulus that never takes an hour off, whether it is 7:00 AM or 7:00 PM.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

