					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>11. Why do conventional toothpastes and mouthwashes make the burning so much&nbsp;worse?</h3>
						<p>Nearly all commercial toothpastes contain foaming detergents such as Sodium Lauryl Sulfate (SLS), strong essential oils (menthol, eucalyptol, peppermint), and acidic preservatives (citric acid or benzoic acid). SLS strips away the protective mucin protein barrier lining the tongue and gums, exposing unmyelinated nerve endings. Meanwhile, mint and menthol bind directly to cold and irritant receptors (TRPM8 and TRPA1) that are already hyper-sensitized by chronic acid exposure, triggering an intense chemical burning sensation rather than freshness. Switching to SLS-free, unflavored, neutral tooth powders or simple saline rinses immediately removes this daily chemical&nbsp;insult.</p>

						<h3>12. Could a hidden root canal infection or failing crown margin trigger burning on the other side of my&nbsp;mouth?</h3>
						<p>Yes. The trigeminal nerve (Cranial Nerve V) branches symmetrically through the jaws, tongue, and palate, but its primary sensory afferents converge centrally in the brainstem within the spinal trigeminal nucleus (specifically the Subnucleus Caudalis). When a microscopic margin defect beneath a crown chronically leaks bacterial metabolic acids and endotoxins into periodontal tissues, it generates an unrelenting barrage of sensory action potentials. Inside the brainstem, synaptic neurotransmitter spillover (volume transmission) and central sensitization can cross-activate adjacent second-order neuronal pools. This shared central processing can project referred scalding sensations to the contralateral tongue border, lips, or opposite arch, even though the primary peripheral trigger is isolated to a single compromised&nbsp;margin.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>87c</span>
				</div>
			</section>

