					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>13. What should dental hygienists and dentists do differently during routine cleanings for a BMS&nbsp;patient?</h3>
						<p>Routine dental cleanings can trigger severe multi-week flares if clinicians use abrasive ultrasonic scalers on exposed root necks, acidic polishing pastes, or high-pressure air dryers on dehydrated dentin. Clinicians should be asked to perform gentle manual hand-scaling without air-drying sensitive cervical collars, avoid all fluoride foams containing acidic flavoring, and immediately apply a biocompatible dentinal tubule desensitizing resin (such as Tokuyama Shield Force Plus or Gluma) over any exposed tooth roots at the conclusion of the appointment to keep the protective peripheral barrier&nbsp;intact.</p>

						<h3>14. If blood tests and oral biopsies are spotless, how can doctors be sure this is a real physical nerve&nbsp;condition?</h3>
						<p>Standard macroscopic pathology tests (such as basic visual tissue inspection or standard H&amp;E stained mucosal biopsies) are calibrated to detect large-scale cellular abnormalities like cancer, fungal ulcers, or autoimmune lesions. They completely lack the resolution to evaluate small, unmyelinated C-fibers or proton-sensing receptor kinetics. Specialized neurological research using PGP 9.5 immunostaining and quantitative sensory testing (QST) consistently demonstrates a 30% to 50% loss of small-fiber nerve density in burning mouth patients along with hyper-reactive thermal thresholds, definitively validating Burning Mouth Syndrome as a verified small-fiber neuropathic condition driven by ongoing peripheral&nbsp;irritation.</p>

